# Mentor forecast handoff: routing interface draft

Owner confirmed 2026-10-03: forecast output file/API not yet delivered.

`forecast_payload.py` validates a draft JSON heat-flux payload and metadata. This is a fixture-tested adapter scaffold, not production forecasting integration.

Required quantities per scenario member, time interval and projected grid cell:
- incident heat flux in kW/m² (nonnegative finite array);
- flame-presence Boolean mask;
- explicit known-support Boolean mask; unknown cells are unusable;
- explicit increasing time breakpoints relative to valid_start, covering the full validity interval;
- stable member identities, source/model identity, grid geometry EPSG:5179, issue and input-availability times;
- payload SHA256 and matching road-graph SHA256;
- destination dwell duration. Threshold policy belongs to routing and must be separately declared.

Tensor order: (member, interval, y, x). Header version WFG-ROUTING-HAZARD-0.1; full fields in existing hazard_contract.py. Units and physical calibration are not established by passing schema checks. Fire detection probabilities, arrival maps, FRP and radiance cannot simply be renamed heat flux. If mentor supplies an arrival/flame forecast instead, an explicitly documented physical heat-exposure adapter is still needed.

ReplanGate forwards absolute decision time, node, incoming edge and per-member remaining exposure budgets to an injected planner. It rejects duplicate/stale issues, unsupported data, mismatching graph/members, and mid-edge replanning without a committed-prefix rule. Existing spent exposure is not reset. It is not bound to the road solver or an API transport yet; there is no claim of live replanning. New scenario identities require a defensible carry-forward rule rather than matching arbitrary member indices.

Next integration gates: agree on actual mentor format; translate fields to exact road occupancy profiles; bind planner with absolute-time and committed-prefix semantics; audit destination dwell beyond arrival; exercise update/reconnect/out-of-order events and real payloads; compare replans against full independent path checks. Dense JSON is for reviewable fixtures; real grids should use a typed array/tile format with the same admission semantics.
